<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Zhabdrung Bhutan</h1><p class="st-meta">1616 CE – 1907 CE · capital Punakha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The state founded by the Tibetan lama Ngawang Namgyal that unified Bhutan under a dual system of religious and civil rule.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a>, a lama of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drukpa-kagyu">Drukpa</a> school, fled <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> in 1616 after a dispute over his recognition as a reincarnation. In western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bhutan">Bhutan</a> he built fortress-monasteries, or dzongs, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simtokha-dzong">Simtokha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punakha">Punakha</a>, repelled Tibetan invasions and unified the valleys, taking the title Zhabdrung.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Zhabdrung</a> created the dual system, chhoesi, in which a chief abbot, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/je-khenpo">Je Khenpo</a>, led the monastic body while a civil ruler, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druk-desi">Druk Desi</a>, governed, with regional governors called penlops. He codified laws and established Bhutanese identity. After his death, kept secret until about 1705, reincarnations of the Zhabdrung served as nominal heads.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Civil wars among penlops weakened central authority, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bhutan">Bhutan</a> lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dooars">Duars</a> lowlands to the British in the war of 1864 to 1865. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a>, penlop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trongsa">Trongsa</a>, emerged supreme, and in 1907 an assembly elected him hereditary king, creating the Kingdom of Bhutan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 1616 CE – 1907 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30 V82 M40 58 H60"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">theocracy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:91.86%;width:5.79%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1616 CE – 1907 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Punakha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ngawang Namgyal, Jigme Namgyal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Drukpa Kagyu Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dzongkha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bhutan">Bhutan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Druk Desi state, Cho-sid Bhutan, Bhutan (1616-1907)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=zhabdrung-bhutan" aria-label="Zhabdrung Bhutan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7039 1165 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zhabdrung Bhutan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7359 1421L7355 1426"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7355 1426h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7359 1421h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7359 1421h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Punakha. <a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=zhabdrung-bhutan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
